# Line endings, so a checkout on Windows builds the same as one on macOS.
#
# Git's own heuristic already treats these files as binary — the sample keystore holds a NUL byte
# at offset 159, well inside the block Git inspects — but the heuristic is not the only thing in
# play. A developer with `* text=auto eol=crlf` in a global config, or a tool that rewrites the
# working tree, converts the 14 lone LF bytes inside that keystore into CRLF and the file grows by
# 14 bytes. Gradle then fails with `Invalid keystore format` on that machine only, from a file the
# diff says nobody touched. Naming them here removes the dependency on per-developer config.
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
*.p12 binary
gradle-wrapper.jar binary

# `gradlew` is run by a Unix shell, including Git Bash and WSL on Windows. Checked out with CRLF —
# which `core.autocrlf=true` does by default there — it dies with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`.
gradlew text eol=lf

# `gradlew.bat` is the mirror case: cmd.exe mis-parses a batch file that has no CRLF. This must be
# `eol=crlf` and not `-text`: the committed blob is LF-only — it was normalised on the way in by a
# `core.autocrlf=input` machine — so "leave the bytes as they are" would hand Windows precisely the
# file that breaks there. `eol=crlf` restores the line endings at checkout whatever the blob holds.
*.bat text eol=crlf
